1.1 WHO IS INVOLVED AND WHAT DO THEY CARE ABOUT?
─────────────────────────────────────────────────

CONTROLLERSHIP TEAM (primary users of this platform):
  - Manages AR (Accounts Receivable): billed, unbilled, on-account
  - Pulls 3 Oracle/Fusion reports manually every month
  - Manually judges how much cash will land in the account this month
  - Current accuracy: ~97-98% (very good but entirely mental-model-based)
  - Pain: no systematic tool — all knowledge lives in people's heads
  - Key-person risk: if those people leave, the process collapses

TREASURY / BANKING TEAM (downstream consumer):
  - Asks controllership: "How much GBP, EUR, USD will we collect this month?"
  - Uses that number to decide how much to hedge on the currency market
  - If forecast is wrong → over-hedge or under-hedge → higher bank premiums
  - Goal: more precise forecast = lower hedging cost = direct company savings

COLLECTION TEAM:
  - Follows up on overdue invoices
  - Needs to know WHICH invoices to prioritize (high overdue, high risk)
  - Today: no systematic prioritization, all manual

1.2 THE AS-IS PROCESS (what they do today manually)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

STEP 1 — Service Delivery
  → Work is done for clients but not yet billed (UNBILLED state)

STEP 2 — Days to Bill (Avg 18 days delay)
  → Team waits for client Purchase Order (PO) or draft invoice approval
  → 18 days of delivered-but-unbilled work sitting idle
  → This directly inflates Days Sales Outstanding (DSO)

STEP 3 — Invoice is Raised → BILLED AR
  → Oracle/Fusion raises the invoice
  → Invoice enters the BILLED AR report with a due date

STEP 4 — Client Pays (or doesn't)
  → Payment arrives on/before/after due date → enters COLLECTION data
  → If no payment: invoice ages through 30d / 60d / 90d / 90d+ buckets

STEP 5 — PARALLEL PATH: Unmatched Receipts → ON ACCOUNT
  [NOTE: This is a PARALLEL path, not sequential after collection]
  → Sometimes a client sends a lump-sum payment that doesn't map to 
    a specific invoice (e.g., parent company paying for 3 child entities)
  → Money sits "on account" — cash is in the bank but AR still shows 
    those invoices as outstanding
  → Finance team manually matches these (small volume, can be large value)

STEP 6 — Monthly Judgment + Treasury Report
  → Controllership team manually reviews all 3 reports
  → Uses past client behavior to estimate: "Client X will pay this week"
  → Converts everything to GBP/EUR/USD millions
  → Sends to treasury/bank for hedging decisions

1.3 THEIR CORE PAIN POINTS
────────────────────────────

PAIN 1 — Forecasting is 100% manual and judgment-based
  → No tool helps systematically estimate who will pay when
  → Smart people carrying institutional knowledge in their heads
  → High key-person dependency

PAIN 2 — DSO validation is backward-looking, not predictive
  → They use DSO gap (e.g., 91 days actual vs 58 days target) to figure out 
    how much to collect — but this is after-the-fact, not a forecast

PAIN 3 — No prioritization of collection efforts
  → Collection team doesn't know which clients/invoices to focus on
  → High-risk clients (disputes, slow payers, Chapter 11) get no special treatment

PAIN 4 — Billing delay of 18 days inflates AR and DSO
  → No system tracking this delay or alerting on it proactively

PAIN 5 — On-account money creates invisible cash
  → Cash received but not matched to invoices means AR overstates the problem
  → Forecasting without accounting for on-account money will double-count

PAIN 6 — Currency hedging risk
  → Any forecast error leads to higher bank premiums for hedging

PAIN 7 — Edge cases are entirely manual
  → ATNA: custom billing rule (counts payment term from 1st of month)
  → Chapter 11 clients: money may never arrive, but still shows in AR
  → Parent-child payments: one receipt covers multiple child invoices
  → Seasonal patterns: end-of-month and quarter-end payment behavior differs


