2.1 WHAT DSO ACTUALLY MEANS (Traditional Definition)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  DSO = (Accounts Receivable / Total Revenue) × Number of Days

  This is a COMPANY-LEVEL health metric. It answers:
  "On average, how many days does the company take to collect invoices?"

  Controllership uses DSO as a VALIDATION CHECK:
  "My historical average DSO is 58 days. My current AR implies a 91-day DSO.
   So I need to collect X more to bring it back to 58."

  → DSO is backward-looking validation. Not a forward-looking prediction.

2.2 WHAT OUR MODEL ACTUALLY PREDICTS
──────────────────────────────────────

  Our model predicts: "For this specific invoice and client, how many days 
  from invoice date will it take them to pay?"

  This is NOT company-level DSO. This is:
  → "Average Payment Days" — per client, per invoice, per entity
  → Technically: "Days to Collect" or "Client Payment Days"

  Confirmed by Thursday session: the team agreed to rename the dashboard 
  metric from "DSO" to "Average Payment Days."

2.3 WHY THIS DISTINCTION MATTERS
──────────────────────────────────

  Traditional DSO = aggregate, backward-looking
  Our model output = per-invoice, forward-looking

  MODEL INPUTS (training): Collection register — which client paid, when, 
  against which invoice, how many days after invoice date, how much.

  MODEL TEST DATA: Current Billed AR — outstanding invoices with invoice 
  dates, due dates, client, entity, currency, amount.

  MODEL OUTPUT: Predicted payment date + predicted payment amount per invoice.

  CURRENT GAP: We predict WHEN. We do NOT yet predict HOW MUCH.
  (Addressed in Section 4.)


