2.1 HOW THE TEAM MANUALLY VALIDATES THEIR FORECAST (Image 2)
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  STEP-BY-STEP (worked example from the notes):

  Start: Last month AR position = £276 million (billed + unbilled everything)
  Add:   Projected revenue for the forecast month
  Check: Average daily revenue = £4 million/day
  If I collect ZERO this month:
    → AR would grow from £276M to £372M (+ one month of revenue)
    → That gives a DSO of 91 days (£372M / £4M per day)
  
  But historical average DSO = 58 days
  Gap = 91 days - 58 days = 33 days
  Amount to collect to close gap = 33 × £4M/day = £132 million (~133M)

  "If I collect £133 million, my DSO will come back to 58 days."
  "That is how I cross-verify my forecast number."

  THEN: Client-level forecast is done first (bottom-up).
  THEN: DSO formula cross-check at company level (top-down validation).
  If both numbers are close → share the forecast upward.
  Current DSO target range: 60–63 days (was 50 historically, now higher).

2.2 WHAT THIS MEANS FOR OUR MODEL
───────────────────────────────────

  Our model must produce two outputs that are consistent:
  A) Bottom-up: Sum of per-invoice predicted collections by date + amount
  B) Top-down: Implied DSO from A must be in the 58–63 day target range

  If they diverge: flag it to the finance lead for manual review.
  Add a "DSO Check" widget in the dashboard:
  → "Your forecast implies DSO of X days. Historical target = 58–63 days."
  → Green if within range, Amber if 5% outside, Red if >10% outside.

2.3 AVERAGE PAYMENT DAYS vs DSO (retained from v2, still correct)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  DSO = (Total AR / Revenue) × Days     → Company aggregate, backward-looking
  Avg Payment Days = days_to_pay per invoice → Per-client, forward-looking
  Dashboard uses: "Average Payment Days" (confirmed by Thursday session rename)


