4.1 HOW HEDGING WORKS (explained in workshop)
───────────────────────────────────────────────

  FUNCTIONAL CURRENCY: US Dollar (USD)
  NON-FUNCTIONAL EXPOSURE: GBP, EUR, AUD, and other currencies

  MONETARY ASSET CATEGORIES that treasury hedges:
  1. Cash in bank (already collected, sitting in account)
  2. AR — client billing (invoiced, awaiting collection)
  3. Unbilled (will become AR, then cash)
  4. Intercompany balances (within group entities)

  EXAMPLE (from EXL Ireland context):
  → Collections happen in Europe in GBP and EUR
  → After June month-end close, treasury hedges the June-end AR balance
  → June-end hedge settlement happens in early July
    (first or second week of July = when hedge matures)
  → Treasury needs the forecast BEFORE month-end to set the hedge size

  TIMING CRITICAL:
  "We are sitting in June. We will be hedging the June-end numbers.
   We have to hedge it before the month end."
  → Our forecast must be available by 23rd of the month at latest
    (the team referenced "sitting on 23rd of June, for 30th of June")

  REAL EXAMPLE:
  "I have £22.8 million GBP. I've collected some £10 million in bank.
   So £33 million is my GBP balance. If GBP and USD rates fluctuate,
   it will impact us." → This is the exposure treasury hedges.

4.2 WHAT OUR FORECAST MUST PROVIDE FOR TREASURY
─────────────────────────────────────────────────

  BY CURRENCY, BY ENTITY, BY DATE:
  → What is today's AR balance in GBP / EUR / USD?
  → What AR will convert to cash by month-end?
  → What unbilled will become AR + cash in next 30/60/90 days?
  → Net position = Cash in bank + Expected collections from AR + Unbilled

  NET AR FORMULA (from Image 7):
  NET AR = AR (Billed) + Unbilled - On Account

  TREASURY REPORT FORMAT (what they actually need):
  Row: Entity | Currency | Opening AR | Expected Collections | Unbilled | 
       On-Account | Net AR | Projected Month-End Cash Position

  NEW ACTION: Add USD equivalent column to every currency row in the output
  → "ADD equivalent to USD number for the table expected collection" (Day-2 note)
  → Use current spot FX rate for conversion


