CLIENT TYPES IDENTIFIED IN WORKSHOP — model must handle all:

TYPE A: Perfect payers
  Example: "Good client — always pay on time, 100% potential"
  → payment_pct = 1.0, late_payment_rate ≈ 0
  → Model: Full payment, on due date

TYPE B: Reliable partial payers (high on-time rate)
  Example: Liberty — "almost 90%, pay on time"
  → payment_pct_avg = 0.80–0.90
  → Two invoices will spill to next month ("two things will be spill over")
  → Model: Partial classification, high payment_pct, small spillover

TYPE C: Aetna — high volume, custom billing rule
  → #1 client by collection volume
  → Custom due date: 1st of next service month + 75 days
  → Getting this wrong = 5–10% total forecast error
  → Model: Custom date override + Full payment (likely high value client)

TYPE D: Disputed / Chapter 11
  → "We cannot remove from our books because it's still code cases going on"
  → Stays on "auto sending list" until settled
  → Model: Classify as Dispute, payment_pct = 0, exclude from cash forecast
  → Show separately in dashboard as "Excluded — Legal"

TYPE E: Remittance clients (Column T)
  → Have sent advance payment notice
  → Certain payment in 1-2 days
  → Manual flag in dashboard → exclude from model prediction
  → Show as "Confirmed Incoming" (separate from model output)

TYPE F: Advance invoice clients (Leap invoices)
  → Invoice raised but service not yet delivered (or spans future months)
  → Count in AR but NOT in revenue
  → Need "is_advance_invoice" flag to handle correctly


